  "source": "Numerics/Mathematics/Optimization/Dynamic/Dijkstra.cs, Network.cs, BinaryHeap.cs; every graph and every expected value scraped verbatim from Test_Numerics/Mathematics/Optimization/Dynamic/DijkstraTesting.cs (SimpleEdgeGraphCost, SimpleNetworkRouting, BidirectionalRouting, DisconnectedNodesTest, MultipleDestSharedPath, DisconnectedComponent, DisconnectedComponent2, TrianglePath).",
  "reference": "Numerics @ 2a0357a. Costs are C# `float`, and every weight in DijkstraTesting.cs is a small integer, so all of these values are exact in both float and double: they are asserted at mode \"equal\" (tolerance 0), exactly as the C# tests assert them (Assert.AreEqual(3f, result[2, 2]) with no delta). Unreachable nodes are the C# float.IsPositiveInfinity checks, spelled \"inf\" here. The three method names map to the three C# entry points: \"dijkstra\" is the free Dijkstra.Solve (its single-destination overload when one destination is given, its int[] overload otherwise, matching which one each transcribed test calls); \"network_solve\" is Network.Solve, which caches the incoming-edge lists in its constructor and forwards to the same free solver; \"network_solve_weights\" is Network.Solve(float[] edgeWeights). The oracle emitter drives the free Dijkstra.Solve for ALL THREE, because the shipped C# Network CANNOT BE CONSTRUCTED -- its constructor sizes both edge caches at max node index instead of max + 1 and indexes one past the end, so every construction throws IndexOutOfRangeException (measured; see docs/upstream-csharp-issues.md and numerics/math/optimization/dynamic/network.hpp note 1, which documents the port's one intentional divergence, `max + 1`). Routing the emitter through the free solver is exact rather than approximate: with the ported constructor fixed, Network.Solve forwards its cached edge lists to that very function, and a patched C# Network was measured returning the free solver's table element for element. The \"triangle_path_network_solve_weights_are_ignored\" case pins a SECOND upstream defect on purpose: Network.Solve(float[] edgeWeights) builds a re-weighted edge array and then passes the STALE cached incoming-edge lists alongside it, and the solver reads its weights out of that cache, so the custom weights have no effect whatever. The case supplies all-ones weights on a graph whose real weights are 1/4/1/10 and pins the ORIGINAL-weight answer; if anyone ever \"fixes\" the port to honor the weights, this case fails loudly. That is also why the user-facing shortest_path() verb in both packages never calls this method.",
